> I've always played as the brutes. Samurai, Valkyrie, and im always a
> little hesitant when deciding if I want to play as a spellcaster.
> Probably because i've never had a good game with them. One of the main
> worries I have is with the spellbooks. I play very cautiously, so I
> often spent more time on levels then I need to and that turn count gets
> way up there early on. And so im worried that im going to wear out my
> spellbooks very early on and not have any way of getting doubles. Is
> this much of a problem if im looking to get play a game consisting of
> hundreds of thousands of turns trying to get an insane score?

You can read any spellbook four times (if you don't polymorph it), whenever
you do that, the turn counter for the expiry of that spell is reset to
20000. So in theory, a spellbook gives you 80000 turns of spell usage. I
don't know what kind of patience gaming you intend to do, but probably you
won't need, for example, the spell of magic mapping while you're doing
pudding farming, so you can reread the book just before you go on
exploring, etc. You may also blank and rewrite spellbooks using a magic
marker. Good sources of spellbooks are chests, boxes and statues, and you
can also pray for them (with appropriate luck management). Some of the most
valuable spellbooks (e.g. magic missile and detect monsters) have a high
probability of being randomly created.

So I'd say if you intend to take considerably more time than 80000 turns
for your ascension, you'll probably have to throw in a sacrifest session to
get spellbooks (preferably before create monster times out) somewhere in
between. Particularly critical books you can create yourself with a marker.

